    <h2 id="s1">The short answer</h2>
    <p>A heli-skiing tour is a guided day, or run of days, in which a helicopter flies you onto untracked mountains to ski long descents no lift could ever reach. A certified mountain guide leads a small group down each run while the helicopter meets you at the bottom and lifts you back up for the next one. It is lift-served skiing with the lift replaced by an aircraft and the crowds removed entirely.</p>
    <p>In Iceland, the tours we arrange run on the <strong>Troll Peninsula in North Iceland</strong> and are usually sold as <strong>three to five-day weeks</strong> based at a four-star fjord lodge. Heliski Travel handles the whole arrangement end to end, and as the authorised agent, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct.</p>

    <h2 id="s2">How a heli-ski day works</h2>
    <p>The rhythm of a heli-ski day is easy to picture once it is laid out. It is active, sociable and surprisingly relaxed between the skiing.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Morning briefing.</strong> The day starts with a weather and avalanche briefing and a check of your BCA safety kit, so everyone flies informed.</li>
      <li><strong>First flight.</strong> The helicopter lifts your small group to the first drop, a ridgeline or summit chosen by the guide for the conditions.</li>
      <li><strong>The descent.</strong> The guide leads the group down a long, untracked run, reading the snow and picking the line as you go.</li>
      <li><strong>The pickup.</strong> The helicopter meets you at the bottom and flies you back up for the next run. Repeat through the day.</li>
      <li><strong>Mountain lunch.</strong> A pause somewhere spectacular, then back to skiing.</li>
      <li><strong>Home for the evening.</strong> A final run and a flight back to the lodge.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A typical day delivers <strong>15,000 to 25,000 vertical feet across 7 to 14 runs</strong>, all of it on snow no one else has touched. For a first-timer's view of the nerves and the highs, read <a href="/field-notes/what-to-expect-first-heliski-day">what to expect on your first heli day</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s3">What a tour includes</h2>
    <p>A heli-ski tour is close to all-inclusive on the mountain, which is what makes the day feel effortless. A tour covers:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Helicopter flying and guaranteed vertical feet</strong>, priced by descent rather than by the day.</li>
      <li><strong>IFMGA/UIAGM-certified mountain guiding</strong> in small groups, typically four guests to a guide.</li>
      <li>A full <strong>BCA avalanche safety kit</strong> per guest, plus <strong>K2 skis or boards</strong> if you need them.</li>
      <li>On multi-day tours, <strong>accommodation and meals</strong> at the four-star Sigló Hótel in Siglufjörður.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>You bring your own boots, helmet and ski clothing, and travel insurance that explicitly covers off-piste and helicopter activity. That is the whole list. For the numbers behind it, see <a href="/heliskiing-guide/heliskiing-cost-iceland">what heli-skiing costs in Iceland</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s4">Day tour or multi-day week</h2>
    <p>Single-day heli tours exist, but multi-day tours are far more common and far more rewarding, and the reason is weather. Heli-skiing depends on flyable conditions, and a longer tour gives the sky more chances to cooperate. A one-day tour is a gamble; a three to five-day week almost always delivers.</p>
    <p>The pricing protects you here. Because tours are sold by <strong>guaranteed vertical feet</strong> rather than by the day, a storm that grounds the fleet carries your vertical over instead of wasting it. That turns a longer tour into a form of insurance: more days, more buffer, more untracked skiing. Add the travel to North Iceland and a proper week makes far more sense than a flying visit.</p>

    <h2 id="s5">Guiding and safety</h2>
    <p>The guide is the heart of any heli-ski tour. An <strong>IFMGA/UIAGM-certified</strong> professional leads your small group all day, carrying out avalanche assessment, choosing terrain to suit the conditions and the group, and making the calls that keep the day both thrilling and sound. Every guest carries a full <strong>BCA safety kit</strong>, and pilots are experienced mountain flyers operating the <strong>AS-350 B3 (Airbus H125)</strong>.</p>
    <p>Heli-skiing is a backcountry activity with genuine, inherent risk that is managed professionally rather than removed. Your part of the bargain is simple: listen to the briefing, ski within the group and follow the guide's calls. For the full picture of how safety works day to day, read our guide to <a href="/heliskiing-guide/heliskiing-safety-guides">guides and safety</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s6">Where the tours run</h2>
    <p>Heli-ski tours run in a handful of great regions around the world, but the ones we specialise in take place on the <strong>Troll Peninsula in North Iceland</strong>. The signature here is the <strong>sea-to-summit descent</strong>: a run that starts on an Arctic ridgeline and finishes near the shoreline of the ocean itself, something you can do in very few places on earth.</p>
    <p>Across <strong>eleven mapped zones</strong> the terrain ranges from forgiving to genuinely steep, and the long spring daylight means more skiable hours per day than a mid-winter destination can offer. If you are weighing destinations, our comparisons of <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-iceland-vs-alaska">Iceland versus Alaska</a> and <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-iceland-vs-canada">Iceland versus Canada</a> lay out the trade-offs.</p>

    <h2 id="s7">What a tour costs</h2>
    <p>Iceland heli-ski tours run <strong>roughly EUR 3,490 to EUR 82,990</strong> depending on length and format, from a shared group on a shorter week to a private helicopter for a full week. The <strong>format moves the price far more than the dates do</strong>, and most guests sit somewhere in the middle.</p>
    <p>The pricing is honest about the one thing no one controls. Tours are sold by guaranteed vertical feet, so weather delays and repositioning are the operator's cost, not yours. That removes the worst way a tour can waste your money. For a full breakdown, see <a href="/field-notes/iceland-heliskiing-cost-breakdown">the Iceland heli-ski cost breakdown</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s8">Who a tour is for</h2>
    <p>A heli-ski tour suits confident off-piste skiers and riders who can link turns in variable snow. You do not need to be an expert, but you should be a <strong>competent, confident intermediate</strong> comfortable on most red terrain off-piste. Guides shape the terrain to the group, so a tour can accommodate a range of abilities within reason.</p>
    <p>If you are newer to skiing off the piste, spend a season building off-piste mileage first, and read <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-for-beginners">heli-skiing for beginners</a> so the tour is a joy rather than a stretch.</p>

    <h2 id="s9">How to book a tour</h2>
    <p>Booking a heli-ski tour should be simple. Tell Heliski Travel your group size, your skiing level, your preferred dates and the format you have in mind, and we will lay out the honest options and arrange the tour end to end. As the <strong>authorised booking agent for Viking Heliskiing</strong>, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct, and we reply within 12 hours.</p>
    <p>When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a>, see the terrain on the <a href="/iceland">Iceland page</a>, or <a href="/contact">request a tailored quote</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s10">Heli tours vs cat-ski and touring tours</h2>
    <p>Heli-skiing is not the only guided backcountry tour on offer, and it helps to know where it sits. A <strong>cat-skiing tour</strong> uses a snowcat instead of a helicopter to reach terrain, cheaper and more weather-resilient, but slower, with fewer runs and gentler access. A <strong>ski-touring tour</strong> has you climb under your own power on skins, the most physical option, and the most self-reliant.</p>
    <p>A <strong>heli-ski tour</strong> sits at the top for access and vertical: the helicopter reaches terrain the others cannot, and delivers the most untracked descents per day. It is the most expensive of the three, but for many skiers the volume and quality of skiing justify it. Iceland also offers a blend, <strong>heli-assisted touring</strong>, which uses the helicopter for the big lift and short skins for the lines. Our full comparison of <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-vs-cat-skiing-vs-touring">heli versus cat versus touring</a> weighs the trade-offs in detail.</p>

    <h2 id="s11">How to choose the right tour</h2>
    <p>Choosing the right heli-ski tour comes down to four questions, and answering them honestly saves money and disappointment.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What is your ability?</strong> Confident intermediates and up will get the most from a heli tour; newer off-piste skiers may prefer to build mileage first.</li>
      <li><strong>How large is your group?</strong> Groups of four or more can make a semi-private or private tour surprisingly cost-effective per person.</li>
      <li><strong>What is your budget?</strong> A shared tour spreads the flying cost; a private tour buys flexibility and exclusivity.</li>
      <li><strong>When can you travel?</strong> The March to mid-June season shapes snow, daylight and pricing.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you are unsure, that is exactly what we are here for. Tell us your answers and we will recommend the tour that fits, with no pressure to over-buy. Start with <a href="/field-notes/how-to-book-a-heliski-trip">how to book a heli trip</a> for the full process.</p>

    <h2 id="s12">What happens on a down day</h2>
    <p>Weather is the one variable no operator can control, and honesty about it matters more than optimism. On a down day, when the mountains are unsafe or the fleet cannot fly, a heli-ski tour does not simply lose the day and your money with it. Because Iceland heli-skiing is priced by <strong>guaranteed vertical feet</strong> rather than by the day, your vertical carries over instead of evaporating.</p>
    <p>Guides put down days to good use, briefings, local exploration, rest, or a short window if one opens, and fly the moment the mountains allow. This is the quiet genius of the vertical-feet model: it turns weather from a financial risk into a scheduling one. On a proper three-to-five-day tour there is almost always enough of a window to deliver your skiing, which is exactly why a longer tour beats a single-day gamble. Read more on <a href="/field-notes/weather-and-down-days-heliskiing">weather and down days</a>.</p>

